.TH GARNER.YAML 5 "" "garner" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
garner.yaml \- garner configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/garner/garner.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR garner (1)
reads one YAML file.
It holds the decisions you make: what to serve, where to sync from and
how to sign.
Everything garner learns while running (packages, tokens, owners,
snapshots) lives in the database instead.
.PP
The default path is
.IR /etc/garner/garner.yaml ;
.B \-\-config
overrides it.
.B garner config validate
.RI [ FILE ]
checks a file.
It works offline and never opens the files that
.B *_file
keys point to, so it can run in CI.
.PP
.B SIGHUP
or
.B garner reload
reloads a running server.
An invalid file is rejected and logged, and the last good configuration
stays active.
See
.B CHANGES THAT NEED A RESTART
for the exceptions.
.PP
Unknown keys are errors, so a misspelled key fails validation instead of
being ignored.
The file must hold exactly one YAML document.
Validation reports every problem at once, each prefixed with its key path,
for example
.BR "sources[2].assets[0].suite: unknown suite \(dqnoble\(dq" .
.PP
Secrets are never written inline.
Point to them with a
.B *_file
or a
.B *_env
key.
.PP
Every key is optional unless marked
.BR required .
A key you leave out keeps its default.
.SH VALUE FORMATS
.TP
.I duration
A number with a unit:
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h .
There is no unit for days.
Examples:
.BR 30m ,
.BR 1h30m ,
.BR 168h .
.TP
.I size
Bytes, or a number with a unit:
.BR KB ", " MB ", " GB ", " TB
(powers of 1000) or
.BR KiB ", " MiB ", " GiB ", " TiB
(powers of 1024).
Examples:
.BR 2GiB ,
.BR 500MB ,
.BR 1048576 .
.TP
.I glob
.B *
matches any run of characters,
.B ?
one character,
.B [...]
a character class
.RB ( [^...]
negates it), and
.B \e
escapes the next character.
Examples:
.BR tool\-* ,
.BR *_amd64.deb .
.TP
.I listen address
.IB host : port
or
.BI : port\fR.
Examples:
.BR :8080 ,
.BR 127.0.0.1:9090 .
.SH SERVER
.TP
.BR listen " (\fIlisten address\fR, default " :8080 )
Where the HTTP server listens.
Changing it needs a restart.
.TP
.BR public_url " (\fIURL\fR, " required )
The URL apt and dnf clients use.
It goes into the client files and the landing page.
Must be an absolute
.B http
or
.B https
URL without a query or fragment.
A path is allowed, for example
.BR \%https://example.com/garner/ .
.TP
.BR client_name " (\fIname\fR, default " garner )
Prefix of the files clients install:
.IB name .sources\fR,
.IB name .repo\fR,
.I /usr/share/keyrings/\fIname\fP.gpg\fR,
and the dnf repo ID.
Lowercase letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
must start with a letter or digit.
.TP
.BR trusted_proxies " (\fIlist of IPs or CIDR prefixes\fR, default " [] )
Reverse proxies whose
.B X\-Forwarded\-*
headers are honoured.
Authenticated requests are accepted only from loopback, or from one of
these with
.BR "X\-Forwarded\-Proto: https" .
Others get 403.
.TP
.BR allow_insecure_auth " (\fIbool\fR, default " false )
Accept authenticated requests over plain HTTP from any address.
Tokens then travel unencrypted, so use this only on an isolated lab
network.
.SH METRICS
.TP
.BR listen " (\fIlisten address\fR, default empty)"
Serve
.B /metrics
on a separate address.
When empty,
.B /metrics
is served on
.BR server.listen .
Changing it needs a restart.
.SH LOG
.TP
.BR format " (" json " or " text ", default " json )
Log line format.
.TP
.BR level " (" debug ", " info ", " warn " or " error ", default " info )
Lowest level that is logged.
.SH DATABASE
.TP
.BR url " (\fIURL\fR, default " \%sqlite:///var/lib/garner/garner.db )
The database.
Only SQLite is supported:
.B sqlite://
followed by an absolute path, so three slashes in total.
Keep the file on local disk, never on NFS.
Changing it needs a restart.
.SH STORAGE
.TP
.BR path " (\fIabsolute path\fR, default " /var/lib/garner )
Root of package blobs, snapshots and
.IR public/ ,
the directory clients are served from.
Changing it needs a restart.
.TP
.BR gc_grace " (\fIduration\fR, default " 10m )
How long a replaced snapshot is kept before deletion, so requests already
resolved through it can finish.
Must not be negative.
.TP
.BR index_history " (\fIduration\fR, default " 1h )
Index files of every snapshot published within this window are carried
into each new snapshot, so a client that fetched the signed top-level file
just before a publish can still fetch the indexes it lists.
The previous snapshot's index files are always carried over.
Must not be negative.
.TP
.BR snapshot_retention " (\fIinteger\fR, default " 30 )
How many snapshots are kept, newest first.
The current snapshot is always kept.
Must be at least 2.
.TP
.BR max_package_size " (\fIsize\fR, default " 2GiB )
Largest package garner accepts, from an upload or as a synced release
asset.
A larger upload gets 413; a larger asset is skipped and recorded as a
conflict.
Must be greater than zero.
.SH SIGNING
.TP
.BR keys " (\fIlist of keys\fR, default " [] )
OpenPGP keys.
All of them are published in
.B /key.asc
and
.BR /key.gpg ;
the first one signs.
When the list is empty,
.B garner init
generates an RSA 4096 key at
.I <storage.path>/signing/key.asc
and garner uses it.
.PP
Each entry of
.BR keys :
.TP
.BR key_file " (\fIpath\fR, " required )
Armored private key.
.TP
.BR passphrase_file " (\fIpath\fR, default empty)"
File holding the key's passphrase.
Leave it empty for a key without a passphrase, which is the default for
unattended signing.
.PP
To rotate keys: add the new key second, wait for clients to fetch the
keyring, move the new key first, then remove the old key.
Promoting a new key re\-signs every rpm package from its original.
.SH DEB
.TP
.BR architectures " (\fIlist\fR, default " [amd64] )
Debian architectures to publish.
Lowercase letters, digits and
.BR \- .
Don't list
.BR all :
architecture\-independent packages are published under every listed
architecture automatically.
.TP
.BR suites " (\fIlist\fR, default " [stable] )
Suites to publish.
Letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- .
The first suite, or
.B stable
if listed, is the default: uploads without
.B \-\-suite
and sources without asset rules use it, and its client file is served at
the root, the others below
.IR clients/<suite>/ .
.SH RPM
rpm support also needs
.BR createrepo_c ,
.BR rpmsign ,
.B gpg
and
.B gpgconf
on
.BR PATH .
Without them
.B serve
disables rpm and logs which tools are missing.
.TP
.BR architectures " (\fIlist\fR, default " [x86_64] )
rpm architectures to publish.
Lowercase letters, digits and
.BR _ .
Don't list
.BR noarch ;
it is implicit, as
.B all
is for deb.
.TP
.BR releases " (\fIlist\fR, default " [stable] )
Releases to publish.
Same rules and default handling as
.BR deb.suites .
.SH DEFAULTS
Settings for every source that does not override them.
.B retention
also applies to uploaded packages.
.TP
.BR retention " (\fIinteger\fR, default " 3 )
Versions kept per package name, distribution and architecture.
Older versions leave future snapshots; pinned packages are always kept.
Must be at least 1.
.TP
.BR poll_interval " (\fIduration\fR, default " 1h )
How often a source is polled, with \(+-10% jitter.
Must be at least
.BR 1m .
.TP
.BR verify " (" opportunistic " or " required ", default " opportunistic )
Checksum policy.
An asset is checked against the forge's digest or the release's checksum
file when one exists, and a mismatch always rejects it.
.B opportunistic
accepts assets without a checksum and marks them unverified.
.B required
rejects them.
.TP
.BR approval " (" auto " or " manual ", default " auto )
With
.BR manual ,
new packages are stored as held and published only after
.BR "garner approve" .
.TP
.BR prereleases " (\fIbool\fR, default " false )
Sync pre\-releases too.
Drafts are never synced.
.SH FORGES
A list of forges that sources sync from.
There are none by default.
.TP
.BR name " (\fIname\fR, " required )
Name that sources refer to.
Lowercase letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
must start with a letter or digit; unique.
.TP
.BR type " (" required )
.BR github ,
.BR forgejo ,
.B gitea
(an alias of
.BR forgejo )
or
.BR gitlab .
.TP
.BR url " (\fIhttps URL\fR, default empty)"
The forge's base URL.
Required except for
.BR github ,
where empty means github.com; set it to use GitHub Enterprise Server.
Must be
.BR https ,
because tokens are sent to it.
.TP
.BR token_env " (\fIvariable name\fR, default empty)"
Environment variable holding the API token.
.TP
.BR token_file " (\fIpath\fR, default empty)"
File holding the API token; surrounding whitespace is trimmed.
Set
.B token_env
or
.BR token_file ,
not both.
With neither, requests are unauthenticated.
.TP
.BR ca_file " (\fIpath\fR, default empty)"
PEM file with the CA certificates that sign the forge's TLS certificate.
TLS verification can't be turned off.
.TP
.BR max_concurrent " (\fIinteger\fR, default " 4 )
Most requests in flight to this forge at once.
Must be at least 1.
.TP
.BR proxy " (\fIURL\fR, default empty)"
Proxy for this forge's requests: an
.BR http ,
.B https
or
.B socks5
URL.
When empty,
.B HTTPS_PROXY
and
.B NO_PROXY
from the environment apply.
.PP
The token is sent only to the forge's own scheme, host and port.
Asset links that point elsewhere are fetched without it.
For a private repository, set
.B url
to the address the forge puts in its asset links
(Forgejo's ROOT_URL, GitLab's external_url);
otherwise the downloads fail without the token and are retried on every poll.
.SH SOURCES
A list of places to sync releases from.
There are none by default.
A source is one repository
.RB ( repo ),
a GitHub or Forgejo organization
.RB ( org ),
or a GitLab group
.RB ( group ).
Set exactly one of the three.
.TP
.BR name " (\fIname\fR, " required )
Unique name, used in logs, metrics, status and
.BI "garner sync " name\fR.
Same rules as forge names.
.TP
.BR forge " (\fIforge name\fR, " required )
The
.B forges[].name
to sync from.
.TP
.BR repo " (\fIowner\fB/\fIname\fR, default empty)"
A single repository.
GitLab paths may have more parts
.RI ( group/sub/project ).
.TP
.BR org " (\fIname\fR, default empty)"
A GitHub or Forgejo organization; every repository in it is synced.
New repositories are picked up on the next poll.
.TP
.BR group " (\fIpath\fR, default empty)"
A GitLab group, synced like
.BR org .
.TP
.BR owns " (\fIlist of globs\fR, default " [] )
Package names this source reserves.
Only this source may deliver them, also against uploads.
Globs of two sources must not be able to match the same name; validation
names an example if they do.
.TP
.BR assets " (\fIlist of rules\fR, default " [] )
Which deb suite or rpm release each asset goes to; see
.BR "ASSET RULES" .
.TP
.BR include " (\fIlist of globs\fR, default " [] )
For
.B org
and
.B group
sources only: sync only repositories whose name (without the owner)
matches one of these.
Empty means all.
.TP
.BR exclude " (\fIlist of globs\fR, default " [] )
Skip repositories whose name matches, and skip assets whose file name
matches.
Matching assets are never downloaded.
.TP
.BR checksums " (\fIlist of globs\fR, default " [] )
File names of a release's checksum files.
They replace the built\-in names
.RB ( checksums.txt ,
.BR *_checksums.txt ,
.BR SHA256SUMS ,
.BR *.sha256 ).
.TP
.BR archived " (\fIbool\fR, default " false )
Also sync archived repositories of an
.B org
or
.BR group .
.TP
.BR forks " (\fIbool\fR, default " false )
Also sync forks in an
.B org
or
.BR group .
.TP
.BR recursive " (\fIbool\fR, default " true )
Walk GitLab subgroups.
Only valid on GitLab
.B group
sources.
.PP
A source can also set
.BR retention ,
.BR poll_interval ,
.BR verify ,
.B approval
and
.BR prereleases ,
which override
.B defaults
for that source only.
The same rules apply.
.PP
Some asset names are always skipped:
.BR *.src.rpm ,
.B \%*\-dbgsym*
and
.BR \%*\-debuginfo* ,
and files without a
.B .deb
or
.B .rpm
extension (or
.B .rpm
when rpm is disabled).
.PP
On its first run, a source syncs only the latest release of each
repository.
Later runs pick up every release published since then.
.SS ASSET RULES
Each entry of
.BR assets :
.TP
.BR match " (\fIglob\fR, " required )
Asset file names this rule applies to.
.TP
.BR suite " (\fIsuite name\fR, default empty)"
Target for
.B .deb
assets.
Must be listed in
.BR deb.suites .
.TP
.BR release " (\fIrelease name\fR, default empty)"
Target for
.B .rpm
assets.
Must be listed in
.BR rpm.releases .
.PP
Set
.BR suite ,
.B release
or both.
Rules are evaluated in order.
The first rule whose
.B match
fits the asset name and that sets a target for the asset's format wins, so
a
.B *
rule with only
.B suite
does not catch rpm assets.
.IP \(bu 2
With no rules at all, every asset goes to the default suite or release:
.B stable
if listed, else the first one.
.IP \(bu 2
With rules, an asset that no rule places is skipped and not downloaded.
.IP \(bu 2
If two assets of one release end up as the same package, version,
architecture and distribution, both are skipped and a conflict is
recorded.
.SH CHANGES THAT NEED A RESTART
A reload applies everything except these keys.
Changing them makes the reload fail with a message naming the keys, and
the running configuration stays active until you restart:
.BR server.listen ,
.BR metrics.listen ,
.BR database.url ,
.BR storage.path .
.PP
A reload restarts only the sources whose effective settings changed,
including inherited defaults and their forge's settings.
The others keep their schedule.
.SH EXAMPLE
A small setup: one internal Forgejo, one repository with separate Ubuntu
and EL9 builds, and a whole organization synced to
.BR stable .
.PP
.RS
.nf
server:
  public_url: https://packages.example.com
  trusted_proxies: ["10.0.0.5"]

storage:
  path: /var/lib/garner

deb:
  architectures: [amd64, arm64]
  suites: [stable, noble]

rpm:
  architectures: [x86_64]
  releases: [stable, el9]

forges:
  \- name: forgejo
    type: forgejo
    url: https://git.example.com
    token_file: /run/credentials/garner.service/forgejo\-token

sources:
  \- name: tool
    forge: forgejo
    repo: platform/tool
    owns: ["tool", "tool\-*"]
    assets:
      \- match: "*_ubuntu24.04*.deb"
        suite: noble
      \- match: "*.el9.*.rpm"
        release: el9
      \- match: "*"
        suite: stable
        release: stable

  \- name: platform
    forge: forgejo
    org: platform
    exclude: ["legacy\-*", "*\-musl*"]
    approval: manual
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR garner (1)
